<div class="tldr"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> winter 2026-27 is a strong El Niño, not a La Niña. NOAA gives a better than 83% chance of a strong to very strong El Niño through January to March 2027. Its winter outlook favors a warmer north and a wetter south from California to Florida. In past strong El Niños, California, Utah, Colorado and the Southwest got more snow, and the Pacific Northwest to the Great Lakes got less.</p></div>

<h2>Is this winter El Niño or La Niña?</h2>
<p>El Niño. NOAA's Climate Prediction Center has an El Niño Advisory in effect. Its October 8, 2026 update said El Niño "continues to strengthen" and gave a better than 83% chance of a strong to very strong event through January to March 2027. The weekly Niño-3.4 ocean index was +2.1°C, and CPC put the odds of a historic event (+2.5°C or more) at 83% for October to December.</p>
<p>CPC also cautions that with an event this big, El Niño-like impacts are more likely "though not guaranteed." Its next updates come out November 12 (ENSO) and November 19 (seasonal outlook); we'll update this page after each.</p>

<h2>What NOAA's winter outlook says (December to February)</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Warmer than average</strong> is favored across the northern US: the Pacific Northwest, Northern Rockies, Midwest and much of the East.</li>
  <li><strong>Wetter than average</strong> is favored across the southern US from California to Florida, and along the East Coast.</li>
  <li><strong>Drier than average</strong> is favored in parts of the Northern Rockies and the Great Lakes.</li>
  <li><strong>Utah and Colorado:</strong> equal chances. The Intermountain West has no tilt either way.</li>
  <li>NOAA's outlook does not forecast snowfall totals. It forecasts the odds of above or below normal temperature and precipitation.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What a strong El Niño has meant for ski regions</h2>
<p>NOAA's Climate.gov looked at snowfall during El Niño winters from 1950 to 2009. The most reliable signal is <strong>below-average snow from the Pacific Northwest to the Great Lakes</strong>. In <strong>strong</strong> El Niños, California, Utah, Colorado, Arizona and New Mexico were among the states with the biggest above-average snow, though that rests on only about 10 strong events.</p>
<table>
  <caption>Ski regions in El Niño 2026-27: the official outlook and the typical pattern</caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Region</th><th scope="col">NOAA outlook (Dec to Feb)</th><th scope="col">Typical strong El Niño</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><th scope="row">Pacific Northwest</th><td>Warmer than average (some of the highest odds in the country)</td><td>Below-average snow; NWS Spokane says milder with below-normal snowfall inland</td></tr>
