    <tr><th scope="row">Utah and Colorado</th><td>Equal chances for precipitation</td><td>Strong El Niños leaned snowy, but the sample is small. Northern Utah is "more of a toss-up," while southern Utah leans wet (Handel). The Colorado Climate Center finds no clear El Niño precipitation signal over Colorado.</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">California and the Sierra</th><td>Wetter than average across the south; warmer in the north</td><td>Among the biggest above-average snow states in strong El Niños; the jet stream steers storms farther south</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Southwest (Arizona, New Mexico)</th><td>Wetter than average; drought expected to improve</td><td>Among the biggest above-average snow states (Sangre de Cristos, Colorado Plateau)</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Northeast</th><td>Wetter along the East Coast; warmer</td><td>No clear Northeast signal; more snow in the Mid-Atlantic and southern Appalachians</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Great Lakes</th><td>Drier in parts</td><td>Below-average snow, part of the most reliable El Niño signal</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>How to plan a ski season around it</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Don't bet the whole winter on a seasonal outlook.</strong> It shifts the odds; individual storms decide your trip. Book refundable lodging where you can, and watch the <%= link_to "7-day forecast rankings", where_is_it_snowing_path %>.</li>
  <li><strong>If you're flexible, lean south.</strong> California, the Southwest and southern Utah have the best historical El Niño odds. See the <%= link_to "best ski resorts in California", best_resorts_path("in-california") %> and <%= link_to "Utah", best_resorts_path("in-utah") %>.</li>
  <li><strong>Pacific Northwest and Northern Rockies skiers:</strong> the outlook favors a warm winter. Higher-elevation resorts hold snow better; check each resort's <%= link_to "powder day odds", powder_study_path %> by month.</li>
  <li><strong>Get alerts instead of guessing.</strong> Snow Way's free powder alerts email you when a big storm lines up at your mountain.</li>
</ul>

